[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe problem:[22m The test runner dynamically loads the shared library ([38;5;10mlibsha2.so[0m) and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m via [38;5;10mdlsym[0m. In the original C code, the function is named [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m but the header uses a macro:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m#define SPX_NAMESPACE(s) SPX_##s
#define prf_addr SPX_NAMESPACE(prf_addr)
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This means the C compiler actually emits the symbol as [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m. The Rust translation defined [38;5;10mpub fn prf_addr(...)[0m as an internal Rust function — it was never exported with C ABI, and it lacked the [38;5;10mSPX_[0m namespace prefix. The test runner failed with [38;5;10mundefined symbol: SPX_prf_addr[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe fix:[22m I added a thin C-ABI wrapper function in [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub extern "C" fn SPX_prf_addr(out: *mut u8, ctx: *const SpxCtx, addr: *const [u32; 8]) {
    let out = unsafe { core::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(out, SPX_N) };
    let ctx = unsafe { &*ctx };
    let addr = unsafe { &*addr };
    prf_addr(out, ctx, addr);
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This exports the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m with the correct C calling convention and raw pointer signature matching what the FFI harness expects ([38;5;10munsafe extern "C" fn(*mut u8, *const Ctx, *const [u32; 8])[0m), then delegates to the existing internal [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m implementation. The underlying hash logic was already correct — it was purely a symbol export/naming issue.[0m[0m
